multi-master
Currently I am doing everything on my master only. My master is currently controlling all my slave machine.
What if my master goes down? Who's going to control? How I am going to execute? That is where in real time we will do this multi-master setup.

In that configuration file, you're going to tell them who is going primary master, their IP address and secondary IP address.

On background, this master is actually keep on connecting with the slave machine. They have their own communication.
We are not doing anything special, or we are not doing anything extra by default. They are having their own communication.
If the communication is not happening, just think in the same way load balancer we are having. Right? Load balancer, 
they are going to always check for the EC2 machine. If it is not going to respond to them automatically, it is going to 
consider that EC2 machine is not a healthy machine. So it is not going to recreate the traffic. How it is happening automatically,
we have told them. Same way here, the moment you set up the multi-master, everything will be done by the primary machine.
But if the primary master machine becomes inactive, if it is not responding means immediately the second is going to take primary position,
and we are not going to do anything. We just going to tell them who is primary machine and who is secondary machine. So by informing this 
itself on the background, there comes a communication automatically. Primary is going to try communication along with the slave machine. 
If the primary is not responding means immediately secondary is going to take primary position. Once a cannot control another slave.
It is not like that. It is going to become a master. No, never you can do one, but never be. In real time we are always multi-master setup.

