For just installing nginx, you normally don't need to restart it. So that notify was unnecessary

Why do we use notify with the configuration?

This makes sense:
- name: Copy nginx configuration
  copy:
    src: nginx.conf
    dest: /etc/nginx/nginx.conf
  notify: Restart nginx

Because:
Configuration changed
        ↓
Nginx needs to reload/restart
        ↓
notify
        ↓
Restart nginx

If the configuration did not change:

Configuration already same
        ↓
No change
        ↓
No notify
        ↓
No restart
So the rule is:  Use notify when a task's change requires another action afterward.



src = source (control machine)
dest = destination (managed server)

If nginx.conf is in another directory, you can give its path:

src: /home/ansible/nginx.conf
dest: /etc/nginx/nginx.conf

For example, suppose your control machine has:

/home/ansible/
├── nginx.yml
└── nginx.conf

Your playbook:

- name: Copy nginx configuration
  copy:
    src: nginx.conf
    dest: /etc/nginx/nginx.conf

means:

Control machine
      |
      | nginx.conf
      ↓
Managed EC2
/etc/nginx/nginx.conf
--------------------------
Why do we use this?  notify

Imagine you change nginx configuration:

- name: Copy nginx configuration
  copy:
    src: nginx.conf
    dest: /etc/nginx/nginx.conf
  notify: Restart nginx

If the configuration changes:
Configuration changed
        ↓
Notify
        ↓
Restart nginx

If the configuration is already the same:

No change
   ↓
No restart


Example 1: Nginx is NOT installed

You run:

- name: Install nginx
  apt:
    name: nginx
    state: present
  notify: Restart nginx

Ansible checks:

Is nginx installed?
       ↓
     NO ❌
       ↓
Install nginx
       ↓
CHANGE HAPPENED ✅
       ↓
notify → Restart nginx

Example 2: Nginx is ALREADY installed
Is nginx installed?
       ↓
     YES ✅
       ↓
Nothing to do
       ↓
NO CHANGE ❌
       ↓
Handler doesn't run

So change does not mean you manually changed something.
